        "## Gaussian Integration by Parts & Stein's Lemma\n",
        "\n",
        "Recall that if $X \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)$, then Gaussian integration by parts gives\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{E}[X f(X)] = \\mathbb{E}[f'(X)]\n",
        "$$\n",
        "for every locally absolutely continuous function $f$ for which the two expectations exist and the Gaussian boundary term vanishes. In particular, the identity holds for bounded absolutely continuous functions with bounded derivative.\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose $X\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1)$. Then $X$ has density $\\phi(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{-x^2/2}$ and $\\phi'(x)=-x\\phi(x)$. Therefore, $x\\phi(x)=-\\phi'(x)$. Let $f$ be absolutely continuous and suppose, for example, that $f$ and $f'$ are bounded. Then\n",
        "$$ \n",
        "\\begin{aligned} \n",
        "\\mathbb E[Xf(X)] &=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}xf(x)\\phi(x)\\,dx=-\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f(x)\\phi'(x)\\,dx. \n",
        "\\end{aligned} \n",
        "$$\n",
        "Applying integration by parts,\n",
        "$$ \n",
        "\\begin{aligned} \n",
        "-\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f(x)\\phi'(x)\\,dx &=-\\left[f(x)\\phi(x)\\right]_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} +\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f'(x)\\phi(x)\\,dx. \n",
        "\\end{aligned} \n",
        "$$\n",
        "Since $f$ is bounded and $\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\phi(x)=0$, we have $\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}f(x)\\phi(x)=0$. Consequently, the boundary term vanishes, giving\n",
        "$$ \n",
        "\\begin{aligned} \n",
        "\\mathbb E[Xf(X)] &=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f'(x)\\phi(x)\\,dx=\\mathbb E[f'(X)]. \n",
        "\\end{aligned} \n",
        "$$\n",
        "Hence\n",
        "$$ \n",
        "\\boxed{ X\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1) \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\mathbb E[Xf(X)]=\\mathbb E[f'(X)] } \n",
        "$$\n",
        "for every bounded absolutely continuous $f$ with bounded derivative. The converse of this identity is also true and forms the foundation of Stein's method.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#lem-stein-lemma}\n",
        "## Stein's Lemma\n",
        "Let $W$ be an integrable real-valued random variable. Suppose that\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{E}[f'(W) - W f(W)] = 0\n",
        "$$\n",
        "for every bounded absolutely continuous function $f$ with $\\sup |f'| < \\infty$, where a measurable version of the a.e. derivative is fixed. Then $W \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Gaussian Tail Bounds\n",
        "A standard normal random variable is very unlikely to be far from zero, and the probability of being at least $t$ standard deviations away decreases essentially like $e^{-t^2/2}$. Let $\\Phi(t) = F_Z(t) = \\mathbb{P}(Z \\le t)$ be the standard normal CDF.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#lem-normal-tail-bound}\n",
        "## Normal Tail Bound\n",
        "For all $t > 0$, $\\Phi(-t) = 1 - \\Phi(t)$ satisfies:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Phi(-t) = 1 - \\Phi(t) \\le \\min\\left\\{ \\frac{1}{2}, \\, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi} \\, t} e^{-t^2/2}\\right\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @lem-normal-tail-bound\n",
        "Because $\\Phi(t) \\ge \\Phi(0) = 1/2$ for $t > 0$, we have $1 - \\Phi(t) \\le 1/2$. For $t > 0$, using the standard normal density $\\phi(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-x^2/2}$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "1 - \\Phi(t) = \\mathbb{P}(Z > t) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_{t}^{\\infty} e^{-x^2/2} \\, dx\n",
        "$$\n",
        "For $x > t > 0$, we have $\\frac{x}{t} > 1$. Therefore:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{align*}\n",
        "1 - \\Phi(t) &\\le \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi} \\, t} \\int_{t}^{\\infty} x e^{-x^2/2} \\, dx \\\\\n",
        "&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi} \\, t} \\left[ -e^{-x^2/2} \\right]_{t}^{\\infty}\\\\\n",
        "& = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi} \\, t} e^{-t^2/2}.\n",
        "\\end{align*}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Since both bounds hold simultaneously for all $t > 0$, their minimum is also a valid upper bound:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "1 - \\Phi(t) \\;\\le\\; \\min\\!\\left\\{ \\frac{1}{2},\\; \\frac{e^{-t^2/2}}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,t} \\right\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "In the following, we use Python to visualize the normal tail bound. The plot shows the standard normal CDF $\\Phi(t)$, the tail probability $1 - \\Phi(t)$, and the upper bound $\\min\\{1/2, e^{-t^2/2}/(\\sqrt{2\\pi} t)\\}$ for $t > 0$."
      ],
