        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{#rem-gaussian-tail}\n",
        "Gaussian tails are exponentially thin: moving a few standard deviations away from the mean makes the probability decay extremely rapidly.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Stein's Differential Equation for the Indicator Function\n",
        "\n",
        "For each fixed $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$, consider the Stein equation, interpreted in the almost-everywhere sense:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f_t'(w) - w f_t(w) = \\mathbf{1}_{(-\\infty, t]}(w) - \\Phi(t)\n",
        "$$ {#eq-indicator-ode}\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#lem-indicator-ode}\n",
        "## Solution and Bounds for Indicator Test Functions\n",
        "For each $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$, @eq-indicator-ode has a unique bounded absolutely continuous solution $f_t$ given by:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f_t(w) = e^{w^2/2} \\int_{w}^{\\infty} e^{-x^2/2} \\left( \\Phi(t) - \\mathbf{1}_{(-\\infty, t]}(x) \\right) dx\n",
        "$$ {#eq-indicator-sol}\n",
        "Furthermore, $f_t(w) = f_{-t}(-w)$. The classical derivative exists away from $w=t$, and a measurable version $\\dot f_t$ may be chosen at $t$ so that the Stein equation holds for every $w$. The solution satisfies\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sup_w |f_t(w)| \\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}, \\quad \\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_w |f_t'(w)| \\le 2.\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @lem-indicator-ode\n",
        "\n",
        "**Step 1: Deriving the solution formula.**\n",
        "\n",
        "Multiplying both sides of @eq-indicator-ode by the integrating factor $e^{-w^2/2}$ turns the left side into a derivative almost everywhere:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{align*}\n",
        "\\frac{d}{dw}\\!\\left[ e^{-w^2/2} f_t(w) \\right]\n",
        "&= e^{-w^2/2} f_t'(w) - w\\,e^{-w^2/2} f_t(w) \\\\\n",
        "&= e^{-w^2/2}\\!\\left( \\mathbf{1}_{(-\\infty, t]}(w) - \\Phi(t) \\right).\n",
        "\\end{align*}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Integrating the absolutely continuous integrating-factor expression from $w$ to $R$ gives:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "e^{-R^2/2} f_t(R) - e^{-w^2/2} f_t(w)\n",
        "= \\int_{w}^{R} e^{-x^2/2}\\!\\left( \\mathbf{1}_{(-\\infty, t]}(x) - \\Phi(t) \\right) dx.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "We seek a **bounded** solution. Since $e^{-R^2/2} \\to 0$ as $R \\to \\infty$ and $f_t$ is bounded, the left boundary term $e^{-R^2/2}f_t(R) \\to 0$. The right-hand side converges because $e^{-x^2/2}$ is integrable on $[w, \\infty)$. Letting $R \\to \\infty$ and rearranging:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "e^{-w^2/2} f_t(w)\n",
        "= \\int_{w}^{\\infty} e^{-x^2/2}\\!\\left( \\Phi(t) - \\mathbf{1}_{(-\\infty, t]}(x) \\right) dx,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "and multiplying by $e^{w^2/2}$ yields @eq-indicator-sol. (One verifies that any other value for the constant of integration would force $f_t$ to be unbounded.)\n",
        "\n",
        "**Step 2: Explicit evaluation by cases.**\n",
        "\n",
        "We use the identity $\\int_a^b e^{-x^2/2}\\,dx = \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,(\\Phi(b) - \\Phi(a))$, so in particular $\\int_a^\\infty e^{-x^2/2}\\,dx = \\sqrt{2\\pi}(1-\\Phi(a))$.\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Case 1 ($w < t$):** Split the integration range at $x = t$. On $[w, t]$ the indicator equals 1; on $(t, \\infty)$ it equals 0:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  \\begin{align*}\n",
        "  &\\int_{w}^{\\infty} e^{-x^2/2}\\!\\left(\\Phi(t) - \\mathbf{1}_{(-\\infty,t]}(x)\\right)dx \\\\\n",
        "  &\\quad= (\\Phi(t)-1)\\!\\int_{w}^{t} e^{-x^2/2}\\,dx \\;+\\; \\Phi(t)\\!\\int_{t}^{\\infty} e^{-x^2/2}\\,dx \\\\\n",
        "  &\\quad= \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,(\\Phi(t)-1)(\\Phi(t)-\\Phi(w)) + \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,\\Phi(t)(1-\\Phi(t)) \\\\\n",
        "  &\\quad= \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\!\\left[\\Phi(t)^2 - \\Phi(t)\\Phi(w) - \\Phi(t) + \\Phi(w) + \\Phi(t) - \\Phi(t)^2\\right] \\\\\n",
        "  &\\quad= \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,\\Phi(w)\\,(1-\\Phi(t)).\n",
        "  \\end{align*}\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  Hence $f_t(w) = \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,e^{w^2/2}\\Phi(w)(1-\\Phi(t))$.\n",
        "\n",
        "* **Case 2 ($w \\ge t$):** For all $x \\ge w \\ge t$ the indicator equals 0, so the integrand simplifies to $e^{-x^2/2}\\Phi(t)$:\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  \\int_{w}^{\\infty} e^{-x^2/2}\\,\\Phi(t)\\,dx = \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,\\Phi(t)(1-\\Phi(w)).\n",
        "  $$\n",
        "  Hence $f_t(w) = \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,e^{w^2/2}\\Phi(t)(1-\\Phi(w))$.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Step 3: Symmetry $f_t(w) = f_{-t}(-w)$.**\n",
        "\n",
        "For $w < t$, we have $-w > -t$, so Case 2 applied to $f_{-t}$ at the point $-w$ gives:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f_{-t}(-w) = \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,e^{w^2/2}\\,\\Phi(-t)\\,(1-\\Phi(-w)).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Using $\\Phi(-t) = 1-\\Phi(t)$ and $1-\\Phi(-w) = \\Phi(w)$ yields $f_{-t}(-w) = \\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,e^{w^2/2}\\Phi(w)(1-\\Phi(t)) = f_t(w)$. The case $w \\ge t$ is analogous.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Step 4: Bound $\\sup_w |f_t(w)| \\le \\sqrt{\\pi/2}$.**\n",
        "\n",
        "For Case 1 ($w < t$): since $\\Phi$ is increasing, $1-\\Phi(t) \\le 1-\\Phi(w)$, so:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "e^{w^2/2}\\Phi(w)(1-\\Phi(t)) \\;\\le\\; e^{w^2/2}\\Phi(w)(1-\\Phi(w)) =: h(w).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "The function $h$ is symmetric ($h(-w) = e^{w^2/2}(1-\\Phi(w))\\Phi(w) = h(w)$) and satisfies $h(w) \\to 0$ as $|w| \\to \\infty$ (since the Gaussian tail bound gives $e^{w^2/2}\\Phi(w) \\le ({\\sqrt{2\\pi}|w|})^{-1}$ for $w < 0$ and $e^{w^2/2}(1-\\Phi(w)) \\le (\\sqrt{2\\pi}w)^{-1}$ for $w > 0$). Differentiating:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h'(w) = e^{w^2/2}\\!\\left[w\\Phi(w)(1-\\Phi(w)) + \\phi(w)(1-2\\Phi(w))\\right],\n",
        "$$\n",
        "which vanishes at $w = 0$ (since $1 - 2\\Phi(0) = 0$). To conclude that this is the global maximum, one must also check the sign of $h'$ away from zero. The standard Mills-ratio inequalities imply $h'(w)\\le0$ for $w>0$; symmetry then gives $h'(w)\\ge0$ for $w<0$. Hence the maximum is:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h(0) = \\Phi(0)(1-\\Phi(0)) = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\cdot\\tfrac{1}{2} = \\tfrac{1}{4}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Thus $f_t(w) \\le \\tfrac{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}{4}$ for $w < t$. Since $\\bigl(\\tfrac{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}{4}\\bigr)^2 = \\tfrac{\\pi}{8} \\le \\tfrac{\\pi}{2}$, we obtain $f_t(w) \\le \\sqrt{\\pi/2}$. By the symmetry from Step 3 the same bound holds for Case 2.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Step 5: Bound $\\sup_w |f_t'(w)| \\le 2$.**\n",
        "\n",
        "The classical derivative has a jump of $+1$ at $w = t$ (the indicator is right-continuous at $t$, so $f_t'(t^-) = f_t'(t^+) + 1$). We bound the derivative away from $t$ first.\n",
        "\n",
        "For **$w > t$** (Case 2), the ODE gives $f_t'(w) = wf_t(w) - \\Phi(t)$, hence:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f_t'(w) = \\Phi(t)\\!\\left[\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,w\\,e^{w^2/2}(1-\\Phi(w)) - 1\\right].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "- *Sub-case $w > 0$:* The Gaussian tail bound $1-\\Phi(w) \\le \\frac{e^{-w^2/2}}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,w}$ gives $\\sqrt{2\\pi}\\,w\\,e^{w^2/2}(1-\\Phi(w)) \\le 1$, so $f_t'(w) \\le 0$ and $|f_t'(w)| \\le \\Phi(t) \\le 1$.\n",
        "- *Sub-case $t < w \\le 0$ (so $t < 0$):* By symmetry of $\\phi$, $1-\\Phi(w) = \\Phi(-w)$ and the tail bound at $-w > 0$ gives $\\sqrt{2\\pi}(-w)e^{w^2/2}\\Phi(-w) \\le 1$, i.e., $\\sqrt{2\\pi}we^{w^2/2}(1-\\Phi(w)) \\ge -1$. Hence $|f_t'(w)| \\le 2\\Phi(t) \\le 1$ (since $\\Phi(t) \\le \\tfrac{1}{2}$ for $t \\le 0$).\n",
        "\n",
        "In both sub-cases $\\sup_{w>t}|f_t'(w)| \\le 1$. By the symmetry $f_t'(w) = -f_{-t}'(-w)$ the same bound holds for $w < t$.\n",
        "\n",
        "At the **discontinuity**, the one-sided derivatives satisfy\n",
        "$$\n",
        "|f_t'(t^-)| = |f_t'(t^+) + 1| \\le |f_t'(t^+)| + 1 \\le 2.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Define the measurable version $\\dot f_t$ at $t$ by\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\dot f_t(t):=t f_t(t)+1-\\Phi(t).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Then $\\dot f_t(w)-wf_t(w)=\\mathbf{1}_{(-\\infty,t]}(w)-\\Phi(t)$ for every $w$, while $\\dot f_t=f_t'$ away from $t$, and $|\\dot f_t(t)|\\le2$.\n",
        "\n",
        "Combining, $\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_w |f_t'(w)| \\le 2$ and $\\sup_w|\\dot f_t(w)|\\le2$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Proof of Stein's Lemma \n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @lem-stein-lemma\n",
        "Fix $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and apply the hypothesis to the bounded absolutely continuous test function $f=f_t$ constructed in @lem-indicator-ode, using the measurable derivative version $\\dot f_t$. The bound in that lemma gives $\\sup|\\dot f_t|\\le2$, so $f_t$ is admissible:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "0 = \\mathbb{E}[\\dot f_t(W) - W f_t(W)].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "By the definition of $\\dot f_t$, the Stein equation states that\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\dot f_t(w)-w f_t(w)=\\mathbf{1}_{(-\\infty,t]}(w)-\\Phi(t)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "for every $w\\in\\mathbb R$. Substituting $w=W$ and taking expectations:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{E}[\\dot f_t(W) - W f_t(W)] = \\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[ \\mathbf{1}_{(-\\infty, t]}(W) - \\Phi(t) \\right].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Finally, by linearity of expectation, $\\mathbb{E}[\\mathbf{1}_{(-\\infty, t]}(W)] = \\mathbb{P}(W \\le t)$ and $\\mathbb{E}[\\Phi(t)] = \\Phi(t)$ (a constant), so:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[ \\mathbf{1}_{(-\\infty, t]}(W) - \\Phi(t) \\right] = \\mathbb{P}(W \\le t) - \\Phi(t).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Chaining the three equalities: $\\mathbb{P}(W \\le t) - \\Phi(t) = 0$. Since $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$ was arbitrary, $F_W(t) = \\Phi(t)$ for all $t$, proving $W \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Kolmogorov Distance\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#cor-kolmogorov-distance}\n",
        "## Kolmogorov Distance Representation\n",
        "Let $W$ be a real-valued random variable with $\\mathbb{E}|W|<\\infty$, and let $Z\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1)$. For the measurable derivative version $\\dot f_t$ defined above:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "|F_W(t) - \\Phi(t)| = |\\mathbb{E}[\\dot f_t(W) - W f_t(W)]|\n",
        "$$\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_{\\text{Kol}}(\\mathcal L(W),\\mathcal L(Z)) = \\sup_{t \\in \\mathbb{R}} |\\mathbb{P}(W \\le t) - \\Phi(t)| = \\sup_{t \\in \\mathbb{R}} |\\mathbb{E}[\\dot f_t(W) - W f_t(W)]|.\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Application to Berry-Esseen\n",
        "For i.i.d. real-valued random variables $X_1,X_2,\\ldots$ with\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb E[X_1]=0,\\qquad \\mathbb E[X_1^2]=1,\\qquad \\mathbb E|X_1|^3<\\infty,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "let $S_n=\\sum_{j=1}^nX_j$ and $W=S_n/\\sqrt n$. A standard Stein-method calculation gives, for the appropriate measurable version of the indicator solution,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "|\\mathbb{E}[\\dot f_t(W) - W f_t(W)]| \\le \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{n}} \\mathbb{E}|X_1|^3.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "This estimate is quoted here rather than proved; its proof uses independence, Taylor expansion, and control of the discontinuity of the indicator solution. Because the indicator function $h = \\mathbf{1}_{(-\\infty, t]}$ is discontinuous, working directly with $d_{\\text{Kol}}$ is technically more involved. This motivates smoothing via Lipschitz test functions.\n",
        "\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "## Wasserstein-1 Distance & Smooth Stein Bounds\n",
        "\n",
        "Instead of the Kolmogorov distance, consider the Wasserstein-1 distance:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_{W_1}(\\mathcal L(W),\\mathcal L(Z)) = \\sup_{\\|h\\|_{\\text{Lip}} \\le 1} \\left| \\mathbb{E}[h(W)] - \\mathbb{E}[h(Z)] \\right|\n",
        "$$\n",
        "where $\\|h\\|_{\\text{Lip}} = \\sup_{x \\neq y} \\frac{|h(x) - h(y)|}{|x - y|}$.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a Lipschitz test function $h$, define $\\Phi(h) = \\mathbb{E}[h(Z)]$ where $Z \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)$. Since Lipschitz functions have at most linear growth, $\\Phi(h)$ is finite. For an integrable real-valued $W$, consider the Stein equation:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f_h'(w) - w f_h(w) = h(w) - \\Phi(h)\n",
        "$$ {#eq-stein-smooth-ode}\n",
        "\n",
        "Taking expectations after evaluating both sides at $W$ gives:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{E}[f_h'(W) - W f_h(W)] = \\mathbb{E}[h(W) - \\Phi(h)] = \\mathbb{E}[h(W)] - \\mathbb{E}[h(Z)]\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {#thm-stein-derivative-bounds}\n",
        "## Stein's Derivative Bounds for Lipschitz Functions\n",
        "Let $h \\in \\operatorname{Lip}(\\mathbb{R})$, with Lipschitz seminorm $\\|h\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}}$. The unique bounded solution to @eq-stein-smooth-ode is:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f_h(w) = e^{w^2/2} \\int_{w}^{\\infty} e^{-x^2/2} (\\Phi(h) - h(x)) \\, dx\n",
        "$$\n",
        "The function $f_h$ is $C^1$, its derivative $f_h'$ is locally absolutely continuous, and $f_h''$ exists almost everywhere. The following bounds hold, with the second-derivative bound interpreted in the essential-supremum sense:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sup |f_h| \\le 2 \\|h\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}}, \\quad \\sup |f_h'| \\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}} \\|h\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}}, \\quad \\operatorname*{ess\\,sup} |f_h''| \\le 2 \\|h\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof Sketch of @thm-stein-derivative-bounds\n",
        "\n",
        "The solution formula and uniqueness follow from the same integrating-factor argument as @lem-indicator-ode, now with the continuous function $h-\\Phi(h)$ in place of the indicator. Lipschitz continuity of $h$ ensures $f_h\\in C^1$ and that $f_h'$ is locally absolutely continuous, so $f_h''$ exists almost everywhere.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Bound $\\sup|f_h| \\le 2\\|h\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}}$.**  \n",
        "Write $h(x) - \\Phi(h) = (h(x)-h(w)) + (h(w)-\\Phi(h))$ to split the defining integral:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "e^{-w^2/2}f_h(w) = \\underbrace{-\\int_w^\\infty e^{-x^2/2}(h(x)-h(w))\\,dx}_{I_1(w)} - \\underbrace{(h(w)-\\Phi(h))\\int_w^\\infty e^{-x^2/2}\\,dx}_{I_2(w)}.\n",
