        "::: {#cor-wasserstein-distance}\n",
        "## Wasserstein Distance Bound\n",
        "Let $W$ be a real-valued random variable with $\\mathbb{E}|W|<\\infty$, and let $Z\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,1)$. Then, writing the laws explicitly,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_{W_1}(\\mathcal L(W),\\mathcal L(Z)) \\le \\sup_{f \\in \\mathcal{H}} |\\mathbb{E}[f'(W) - W f(W)]|\n",
        "$$\n",
        "where $\\mathcal{H}$ consists of bounded absolutely continuous functions with a measurable derivative version satisfying\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sup |f|\\le2,\\qquad \\sup |f'|\\le\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}},\\qquad \\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}|f''|\\le2.\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.proof}\n",
        "## Proof of @cor-wasserstein-distance\n",
        "\n",
        "Fix a function $h$ with $\\|h\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}}\\le1$. Since $W$ and $Z$ are integrable and $h$ has at most linear growth, both $h(W)$ and $h(Z)$ are integrable. Let $f_h$ be the bounded solution of the Stein equation @eq-stein-smooth-ode:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "f_h'(w)-wf_h(w)=h(w)-\\Phi(h),\n",
        "\\qquad \\Phi(h)=\\mathbb E[h(Z)].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "By @thm-stein-derivative-bounds, $f_h$ is $C^1$, $f_h'$ is locally absolutely continuous, and\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sup_w|f_h(w)|\\le2\\|h\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\sup_w|f_h'(w)|\\le\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi}}\\|h\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}},\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_w|f_h''(w)|\\le2\\|h\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Because $\\|h\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}}\\le1$, the function $f_h$ belongs to $\\mathcal H$; its continuous derivative $f_h'$ is a measurable derivative version. In particular, $f_h(W)$ and $Wf_h(W)$ are integrable, since $f_h$ is bounded and $W$ is integrable.\n",
        "\n",
        "Evaluating the Stein equation at $W$ and taking expectations gives\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\begin{aligned}\n",
        "\\mathbb E[h(W)]-\\mathbb E[h(Z)]\n",
        "&=\\mathbb E[h(W)-\\Phi(h)] \\\\\n",
        "&=\\mathbb E[f_h'(W)-Wf_h(W)].\n",
        "\\end{aligned}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Consequently,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\left|\\mathbb E[h(W)]-\\mathbb E[h(Z)]\\right|\n",
        "\\le \\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal H}\\left|\\mathbb E[f'(W)-Wf(W)]\\right|.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "This inequality holds for every $h$ with $\\|h\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}}\\le1$. Taking the supremum over all such $h$ and using the definition of $d_{W_1}$ yields\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_{W_1}(\\mathcal L(W),\\mathcal L(Z))\n",
        "\\le \\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal H}\\left|\\mathbb E[f'(W)-Wf(W)]\\right|,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "as claimed.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Takeaways\n",
        "\n",
        "**1. Stein's characterization of the Gaussian.**  \n",
        "A real-valued integrable random variable $W$ is $\\mathcal{N}(0,1)$ if and only if $\\mathbb{E}[f'(W) - Wf(W)] = 0$ for all functions in the specified admissible class, with the chosen measurable derivative versions. The forward direction is Gaussian integration by parts; the converse (@lem-stein-lemma) is proved by constructing the specific test functions $f_t$.\n",
        "\n",
        "**2. The Stein ODE as a bridge.**  \n",
        "The equation $f'(w) - wf(w) = h(w) - \\mathbb{E}[h(Z)]$ converts a distributional comparison (does $W$ look like $Z$?) into an analytic question (how far is $W$ from satisfying the Gaussian IBP identity?). Crucially, taking $\\mathbb{E}[\\cdot]$ at $W$ on both sides gives\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathbb{E}[h(W)] - \\mathbb{E}[h(Z)] = \\mathbb{E}[f_h'(W) - Wf_h(W)],\n",
        "$$\n",
        "so distances generated by such admissible test functions can be bounded by controlling the Stein operator $\\mathbb{E}[f_h' - Wf_h]$.\n",
        "\n",
        "**3. Derivative bounds are the key to Berry-Esseen.**  \n",
        "The utility of the Stein ODE depends on the regularity of its solution. The bounds $\\sup|\\dot f_t| \\le 2$ (indicator case) and $\\sup|f_h'| \\le \\sqrt{2/\\pi}$, $\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}|f_h''| \\le 2$ (Lipschitz case) are what allow the expectation $\\mathbb{E}[f_h'(W) - Wf_h(W)]$ to be estimated from structural properties of $W$ (e.g., independence or moment conditions).\n",
        "\n",
        "**4. Kolmogorov vs. Wasserstein.**  \n",
        "The indicator solutions $f_t$, together with the measurable derivative versions $\\dot f_t$, give the Kolmogorov distance representation:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_{\\mathrm{Kol}}(\\mathcal L(W),\\mathcal L(Z)) = \\sup_{t}\\,|\\mathbb{E}[\\dot f_t(W)-Wf_t(W)]|.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "The Lipschitz solutions $f_h$ give the Wasserstein-1 bound:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "d_{W_1}(\\mathcal L(W),\\mathcal L(Z)) \\le \\sup_{f\\in\\mathcal{H}}\\,|\\mathbb{E}[f'(W)-Wf(W)]|.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Working with $d_{W_1}$ is technically cleaner because the Lipschitz solutions are $C^1$ and $f_h'$ is continuous, while the indicator solutions have a discontinuity in their classical derivative at $w=t$ that complicates estimates.\n",
        "\n",
        "**5. The overall strategy of Stein's method.**  \n",
        "To bound $d(W, Z)$ for a given $W$:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Express the distance as $\\sup_h |\\mathbb{E}[h(W)] - \\mathbb{E}[h(Z)]|$.\n",
        "2. Solve the Stein ODE for each $h$ to obtain $f_h$.\n",
        "3. Rewrite using the Stein operator: $|\\mathbb{E}[h(W)] - \\mathbb{E}[h(Z)]| = |\\mathbb{E}[f_h'(W) - Wf_h(W)]|$.\n",
